        {
            "table": "tt_content",
            "uid": 1401,
            "expect": {
                "CType": "text",
                "header": "Why Walk the Camino?",
                "bodytext": "<p><strong>A Personal Journey&nbsp;</strong><br />Many pilgrims describe the Camino as a life-changing experience. Walking day after day creates space for reflection, clarity, and personal growth — a rare opportunity to step away from the noise of everyday life.</p>\n<p><strong>History and Culture&nbsp;</strong><br />The routes are lined with Roman roads, medieval bridges, ancient churches, and UNESCO World Heritage sites, offering a rich journey through centuries of European history and tradition.</p>\n<p><strong>Community and Connection&nbsp;</strong><br />On the Camino, you are never truly alone. Friendships form naturally, languages mix freely, and shared meals around a table become memories that last long after the journey ends.</p>\n<p><strong>Nature and Simplicity&nbsp;</strong><br />From vineyards and forests to mountain passes and rugged coastlines, the Camino offers a chance to reconnect with the natural world — and with yourself.</p>"
            },
            "set": {
                "header": "Why walk the Camino?",
                "bodytext": "<p><strong>Time to reflect</strong><br />Many pilgrims say the Camino changed their lives. Walking day after day gives you time to think, away from everyday noise.</p>\n<p><strong>History and culture</strong><br />The routes pass Roman roads, medieval bridges, old churches and UNESCO World Heritage Sites. You walk through centuries of European history.</p>\n<p><strong>Community</strong><br />You are rarely alone on the Camino. Friendships form quickly, languages mix and shared meals become lasting memories.</p>\n<p><strong>Nature and simplicity</strong><br />Vineyards, forests, mountain passes and coastlines: the Camino gives you time outdoors at a slower pace.</p>"
            },
            "_context": {
                "page": 99,
                "pageTitle": "Camino",
                "element": 1401,
                "type": "text",
                "roles": {
                    "header": "headline",
                    "bodytext": "body"
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "table": "tt_content",
            "uid": 1400,
            "expect": {
                "CType": "text",
                "header": "What to Expect on the Camino",
                "bodytext": "<p>Daily walks typically range from 15 to 30 km, with well-waymarked paths guided by yellow arrows and scallop shells. Accommodation options are plentiful, from affordable albergues to guesthouses and hotels, and the food along the way is simple, hearty, and full of regional character. More than anything, the Camino invites you into a slower, more mindful pace of life.</p>\n<p>No special training is required — just preparation, curiosity, and an open heart.</p>"
            },
            "set": {
                "header": "What to expect on the Camino",
                "bodytext": "<p>Most walkers cover 15 to 30 km a day. Yellow arrows and scallop shells mark the paths. You can sleep in cheap albergues, guesthouses or hotels. The food is simple, filling and regional, and life runs at a slower pace.</p>\n<p>You don't need special training. You need some preparation and curiosity.</p>"
            },
            "_context": {
                "page": 99,
                "pageTitle": "Camino",
                "element": 1400,
                "type": "text",
                "roles": {
                    "header": "headline",
                    "bodytext": "body"
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "table": "tt_content",
            "uid": 1399,
            "expect": {
                "CType": "text",
                "header": "Preparing for the Camino",
                "bodytext": "<p><strong>When to Go</strong></p>\n<p>Spring (April to June) is a favourite time to walk, with mild weather and blooming landscapes along the way. Autumn (September to October) offers cooler temperatures and fewer crowds, making it another excellent choice. Summer is popular but can be hot and busy, while winter is quiet and reflective — rewarding for those prepared for more challenging conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>What to Pack</strong></p>\n<p>Good preparation starts with comfortable walking shoes and a lightweight backpack. Bring weather-appropriate clothing and don't forget your Pilgrim Credential, used to collect stamps along the way and access pilgrim accommodation.</p>\n<p>Less is more on the Camino.</p>"
            },
            "set": {
                "header": "Preparing for the Camino",
                "bodytext": "<p><strong>When to go</strong></p>\n<p>Spring (April to June) is a favourite time, with mild weather and flowers along the way. Autumn (September to October) is cooler and less crowded. Summer is busy and can be hot. Winter is quiet but harder, so come prepared.</p>\n<p><strong>What to pack</strong></p>\n<p>Start with comfortable walking shoes and a light backpack. Bring clothes for the weather and your Pilgrim's Credential. You collect stamps in it, and you need it to stay in pilgrim accommodation.</p>\n<p>Pack as little as you can.</p>"
            },
            "_context": {
                "page": 99,
                "pageTitle": "Camino",
                "element": 1399,
                "type": "text",
                "roles": {
                    "header": "headline",
                    "bodytext": "body"
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "table": "tt_content",
            "uid": 1398,
            "expect": {
                "CType": "text",
                "header": "Santiago de Compostela",
                "bodytext": "<p>Arriving in <strong>Santiago de Compostela</strong> is a moment many pilgrims never forget. The main square fills with walkers from all over the world, coming together to celebrate, reflect, and attend the Pilgrim's Mass at the magnificent cathedral.</p>\n<p>Here you receive your <strong>Compostela Certificate</strong>, the official recognition of your journey. But for many, reaching Santiago is not an ending — it is the beginning of something that continues long after the walk is over.</p>"
            },
            "set": {
                "header": "Santiago de Compostela",
                "bodytext": "<p>Many pilgrims remember their arrival in <strong>Santiago de Compostela</strong> for years. Walkers from all over the world meet in the main square to celebrate, rest and attend the Pilgrim's Mass in the cathedral.</p>\n<p>Here you receive your <strong>Compostela Certificate</strong>, the official record of your pilgrimage. For many, the effect of the walk lasts long after it ends.</p>"
            },
            "_context": {
